    "result_text": "Tests green (`Tests run: 2, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 `BUILD SUCCESS`). Here's how I addressed each finding:\n\n**Finding #1 \u2014 controller reaching into the `@AfterMapping` hook: fixed.** The controller no longer calls `ownerMapper.composeAddress(...)`. `composeAddress` is now purely the mapper's internal lifecycle hook (create path only); the update path calls the shared `AddressNormalizer.compose(...)` helper directly. The unnatural controller\u2192mapper-internals seam is gone.\n\n**Finding #2 \u2014 duplicated/double normalization: fixed.** Extracted the \"prefer structured line1(+line2), else flat\" choice into a single reusable `AddressNormalizer.compose(line1, line2, flat)` that operates on *already-normalized* values (it only chooses and joins, never re-normalizes). Both callers now normalize each field exactly once:\n- Mapper `@AfterMapping` reads the already-normalized `addressLine1`/`addressLine2` off the target and composes with the normalized flat address.\n- Controller normalizes `addressLine1`/`addressLine2` once (setting them on the entity), then composes from those same normalized values.\n\nOn the \"cleaner still\" sub-suggestion (a MapStruct `void update(dto, @MappingTarget owner)`): I deliberately left the update path as an explicit field-by-field copy. A generated update mapping would also map fields the current update intentionally leaves untouched (`registrationDate`, `birthDate`, etc.), silently changing update behaviour. The shared helper gets the reuse benefit without that risk.\n\n**Finding #3 \u2014 inline fully-qualified annotations: fixed.** Added `import org.mapstruct.AfterMapping;` and `import org.mapstruct.MappingTarget;`, and the annotations now read `@AfterMapping` / `@MappingTarget` to match the file's convention.",
