    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe additive pieces are mostly clean and idiomatic: `AddressValidation`/`AddressValidator` mirror the existing `PostcodeValidation`/`PostcodeValidator` pair exactly, the schema/DTO/model additions are straightforward, and syncing the persisted flat `address` to the composed structured value (so downstream readers like the household hash/locality need no change) is a coherent approach. The problem is concentrated in how the update path reuses the mapper.\n\n**Findings**\n\n1. **`composeAddress` is doing double duty as a MapStruct lifecycle hook and a controller-invoked helper (`OwnerMapper.java:242`, `OwnerRestControllerV1.java:173`).** It is annotated `@org.mapstruct.AfterMapping` \u2014 a callback the generated mapper invokes as part of `toOwner(...)` \u2014 yet the controller reaches into it directly for the update flow. An `@AfterMapping` method is an internal mapping lifecycle hook, not a general-purpose public API; invoking it by hand from a controller couples the controller to the mapper's internals and reads as an unnatural seam. The create path (`addOwner` \u2192 `ownerMapper.toOwner`) triggers it correctly; the update path bolts it on.\n\n2. **The address composition logic is duplicated / re-computed on the update path (`OwnerRestControllerV1.java:171-173`).** Lines 171-172 normalize `addressLine1`/`addressLine2` and set them, then `composeAddress` re-reads the *raw* DTO fields and normalizes `addressLine1`/`addressLine2` a second time internally. The same normalization runs twice, which is the symptom of the composition logic living in the wrong unit for reuse.\n\n   Suggested direction: extract the \"prefer structured line1(+line2), else flat\" composition into a single reusable unit that operates on already-normalized values \u2014 e.g. a small static helper alongside `AddressNormalizer`, or a method on `Owner` \u2014 and have both the mapper's `@AfterMapping` and the controller call that one method. Cleaner still would be a MapStruct update method (`void update(OwnerFieldsDto dto, @MappingTarget Owner owner)`) so the update path runs the same generated mapping (including the `@AfterMapping`) instead of hand-copying each field.\n\n3. **Minor: inconsistent annotation style (`OwnerMapper.java:242-243`).** `@org.mapstruct.AfterMapping` and `@org.mapstruct.MappingTarget` are written as inline fully-qualified names while the rest of the file imports its MapStruct annotations (`Mapping`, `Mapper`, `Named`). Add imports to match the file's convention.",
